diff --git a/specs/designs/numeric-conversion-normalization.md b/specs/designs/numeric-conversion-normalization.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..16c7f3fac4553da7ae2dec33c8cdbe44152a4fc7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/specs/designs/numeric-conversion-normalization.md
@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+# Canonical checked conversion normalization
+
+Named extraction disables only the pinned compiler's opportunistic small-body
+inlining. Explicit inlining remains enabled, including Option.bind. This keeps
+ordinary named helper closures in the established first-order subset; it does
+not recognize specialized names or admit runtime closures.
+
+Before type discovery and lowering, a single pass reconstructs closed, safe,
+total dictionary constants/projections used by checkedConvert. Reconstruction
+alone gives no permission to erase eager computation. The pass checks their
+kernel types and definitionally compares source integer interpretation and the
+target minimum, maximum, and fromInt to the built-in scalar operations/ranges.
+It independently checks the actual operand and Option result types. Dictionary
+spelling is irrelevant. Open, altered, ill-typed, unsafe, or partial inputs are
+refused. Independently, an eager value check must succeed before either
+dictionary is marked consumed. A validated conversion retains only its scalar
+operand in LCNF; the existing typed checked-convert IR operation enforces target
+bounds.
+
+The erasability check admits dictionary construction, function references,
+projections of inert constructors, and pure aliases composed of these forms.
+It inspects safe definition bodies rather than trusting their safety metadata.
+Every application argument and let value is checked **before** beta reduction,
+substitution, or projection can discard it. An alias returning a function must
+also be inert up to the lambda; the lambda body is delayed until application.
+Opaque function references may be stored as values, but calls to them and opaque
+scalar/dictionary thunks are refused. Types and proofs are erased.
+
+The evaluator only constructs literal scalar data: Nat literals at most
+`2^64 - 1`, Int constructors, and negation of an already literal Int within the
+same magnitude representation bound. This admits elaborated positive and
+negative dictionary bounds. The `OfNat` and `Neg` constructor wrappers are
+inspected, including their arguments; no user numeral/negation instance is
+trusted by name or definitional equality. Recursors, branches, `Nat.succ`
+applications, bounded arithmetic, and unclassified calls are refused, even when
+kernel reduction would produce canonical fields. A fuel limit of 256 refuses
+deeper alias graphs rather than assuming they are inert. The evaluator is not a
+general constant folder and intentionally rejects some harmless computations.
+
+Backward liveness removes only the validated **inert** dictionary graph consumed
+by those calls and unused elsewhere. The eager check reconstructs and inspects
+every dependency before consumption, so following the deleted lets' arguments
+cannot newly authorize an unchecked scalar computation. It retains other uses
+and unsupported generic type declarations. It must not perform generic dead-code
+elimination: a separately inserted unused bounded-Nat addition overflow must
+replay as AddOverflow in native and Wasm generated code. Type discovery and body
+lowering receive the same normalized declaration, and coverage reports retained
+unsupported types.
+
+The consumed-dependency regressions insert calls after LCNF extraction, then
+use production `emitKernelIr` (normalization, type discovery, and lowering).
+They assert exactly `noncanonical checkedConvert non-inert dictionary dependency`
+for (1) `canonicalAfter (maxNat + oneNat)`, (2) the same call through a function
+alias, (3) a closed dictionary alias hiding the scalar argument behind another
+safe alias, and (4) a closed alias that branches on that overflow before returning
+a canonical dictionary. Here `maxNat = 2^64 - 1` and `oneNat = 1`. Static kernel
+reduction selects canonical fields in all four cases; the tests require field
+validation to succeed before the exact erasure refusal. The pre-fix RED
+shape is static: the old pass accepts and prunes these computations; this has
+not been executed locally. These graphs are deliberately refused, so there is
+no generated native/Wasm success claim for them. The separate unused-overflow
+fixture remains admitted and its existing generated native/import-free Wasm
+replay at optimization levels 0 and 2 still requires `AddOverflow`.
+
+This is a local argument about eager construction and deletion of recognized
+closed dictionary graphs. Function bodies stored in fields are not executed
+merely to construct a dictionary; the existing field-equivalence check remains
+kernel definitional equality. This change does not establish that arbitrary
+definitionally equal function bodies preserve bounded-target evaluation failures.
+It is not a mechanized universal compiler-equivalence theorem or proof that entire
+numeric roots cannot fail. Existing generic Int/closure/array refusals and UOR
+guards remain in place. Compiler-owned fixtures are test inputs, not substituted
+application Lean or handwritten target implementations.
+
+The repair has no local Lean SDK execution evidence. It requires fresh pinned-SDK
+compilation, regression results, and independent review. See
+scripts/check-numeric-source-stage.sh for the full source-stage command contract;
+every failed gate remains recorded.
